text:
Wait — there's one problem with the steering: `charForward` reads `__CHAR__.rot`. After the yaw leg it exists. OK.

But another subtle point: the steering loop uses the prompt as a sensor. If the character is outside the 5 m enter radius AND outside the cone, no prompt will be displayed at all. Steering via the cross product handles that. But what if the character is inside the 3 m near zone but outside the cone? The 'Enter ship' prompt requires the cone. No problem.

One more consideration: while steering in circles at a distance greater than 5 m, the prompt stays hidden. 30 iterations × ~1.2 sec ≈ 36 seconds. Bearing correction: each nudge is 150 ms × 3 rad/s ≈ 8°. If the initial bearing error is up to 180°, that's up to 22 nudges — it fits.

But note: the initial bearing from the mining point to the ship. Mining point ≈ spawn + ~7 m in the +Z direction. Ship ≈ spawn − 2.5 m on the side (which side? "the character spawns 2.5 m to the side of the ship"). The character's orientation is 69° yawed from +Z. The bearing from the character to the ship is roughly −(7*facing0 + 2.5*side). The angle between the orientation and that bearing is probably ~110–140°. So about 15 nudges ≈ 18 seconds, then the prompt appears (within 5 m? no! Steering doesn't close the distance — when the ship enters the cone at 9 m, the prompt is hidden (outside the 5 m enter radius!).

Hmm! The ship's interactables: the 'Enter ship' prompt's radius is 5 m; the 'Open cargo' zone is 3–5 m. If the ship is 9 m away, the prompt is hidden even when the orientation is aligned. So the steering loop just keeps steering (text is null) forever — 30 iterations is used up, and the final expect fails!

We need a movement phase: once the bearing is roughly aligned (the ship is within the cone), walk W to close the distance. How do we know we're aligned? The ship's bearing is within ±30° of the orientation. We can compute that in Node: angle = the signed angle between the orientation and the to-ship direction; if |angle| < 25° → W burst. Otherwise, nudge with yaw.

A cleaner loop:
```ts
for (let i = 0; i < 60; i++) {
  const text = await promptText();
  if (text === '[E] Enter ship') break;
  const p = await charPos(page);
  const f = await charForward(page);
  const dx = shipPos.x - p.x, dz = shipPos.z - p.z;
  const dist = Math.hypot(dx, dz);
  const angle = Math.atan2(f.z * dx - f.x * dz, f.x * dx + f.z * dz); // signed, in orientation frame: positive = ship is on the right
  if (Math.abs(angle) < 0.45 && dist > 3.2) {
    // aligned: close the distance (up to 5 m → Open cargo zone)
    await touch(page, 'move', { thrust: 1 });
    await page.waitForTimeout(Math.min(400, Math.max(120, (dist - 3.5) * 110)));
    await touch(page, 'move', { thrust: 0 });
  } else if (text === '[E] Open cargo') {
    // in the cone, in the near zone: a small W burst
    ...
  } else {
    const dir = angle > 0 ? 1 : -1;
    await touch(page, 'move', { yaw: dir });
    await page.waitForTimeout(150);
    await touch(page, 'move', { yaw: 0 });
  }
  await charSettled(page);
}
```

Hmm, wait: `angle = atan2(cross, dot)` — the signed angle from the orientation to the to-ship direction; cross>0 = ship is on the right (per the previous analysis). 'd' (yaw +1) turns right. So dir = sign(angle).

W burst duration: at 3 u/s, closing (dist − 3.5) m takes (dist−3.5)/3 seconds. At 9 m: 5.5/3 ≈ 1.8 sec. Use a max of 2000 ms. Then re-evaluate; once dist < 5 m, the 'Open cargo' prompt appears (inside the cone), and once < 3 m, 'Enter ship' appears.

Simplified:
